WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.170
- So, well, today is
dedicated to rock and roll.

00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:04.850
And we've asked Jon Bon Jovi earlier,

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:07.300
what defines rock music for you?

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:11.360
- Well to me, rock and roll
has always been the music

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.350
that we aren't supposed to
listen to as youth, you know.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:16.010
It was,

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:20.530
it started with being, you know,
it's very sexual in nature.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:22.950
It's about the taboos

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.980
and it's about breaking the social norms.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.980
And it's as if you watch how a
rock and roll sort of changed

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:34.020
over time, and we sort
of changed over time,

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.780
now, I consider your hip hop and rap

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.100
sort of rock and roll now
because it's the music

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.932
that you don't want your
kids to listen to, you know?

00:00:41.932 --> 00:00:43.310
- (laughs) Yeah.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:44.820
So you, you're such a mom.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.830
So who were your musical
inspirations growing up?

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:49.518
Who were people you weren't
supposed to listen to

00:00:49.518 --> 00:00:51.070
and you wanted to become? (laughs)

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:52.240
- Right. (laughs)

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.520
Well, Bruce Springs, you
know, Bruce Springsteen was,

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:56.987
he just seemed to have the answers for me,

00:00:56.987 --> 00:00:59.540
as far as rock and roll,
you know, was going.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.540
I probably ended up somewhere
between Janis Joplin

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.140
and Bruce Springsteen, you know? (laughs)

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.280
- Oh my god, I remember that time,

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:05.320
I think it was on the Grammys,

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.380
and you covered "A Piece Of My Heart".

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:08.960
Holy moly.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.000
I was like, so I kind of wanted to quit,

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.750
'cause I was like, (laughs) well,

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:15.600
I'm never gonna do anything like that.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.430
Like, it was just, it was amazing.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:20.880
And it, it just was one of
those times I feel like when,

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.350
we all love singing,
we all love performing,

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:23.480
but there's times when like

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:24.760
you don't even remember what happened

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.325
and you become the vessel

00:01:26.325 --> 00:01:27.158
for something so much
bigger than yourself.

00:01:27.158 --> 00:01:29.930
And that just felt like
that to me watching it.

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:31.700
- It was a very healing moment for me.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.813
I was bald from chemotherapy.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.980
I had gone through that and
it was my first time on stage,

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.410
or even in front of people.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:41.560
I hadn't even been in crowds.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.420
I was still doing radiation treatments.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:47.730
And it meant a lot to me
to get up, and it was,

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:50.093
it was a big changing moment in my life.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.680
It's kind of before that Grammys and after

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.450
is kinda my musical career. (laughs)

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:56.290
- Yeah.

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:57.510
- And yeah, that was,

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:00.960
that was one of my top five moments ever.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.680
- Oh my god. Well, that's
so funny 'cause literally,

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.440
we have shared the stage
before and I was freaking out,

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.100
putting my best Meryl Streep on,

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.515
acting like I was like totally fine.

00:02:10.515 --> 00:02:11.348
- Yeah.

00:02:11.348 --> 00:02:12.181
Oh, it's so sweet.

00:02:12.181 --> 00:02:13.640
And it's very important to me as,

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:16.200
as I've gone through this, my career,

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.950
to really reach out to the
women who are coming up

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.140
and making a name and you were just,

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.070
you were just hitting your stride.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:25.390
You were really doing great.

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:27.430
And you know, you also hold

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:30.290
that sort of rock and roll torch,

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:32.730
but also you're just a
down to earth person.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:33.660
- Ah, thank you.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:35.427
That means a lot coming from you.

